Tweak disassembly settings with 'el asm' to list their descriptions.
You can add commands into your ~/.rizinrc to customize your experience with Rizin
In visual mode press 'c' to toggle the cursor mode. Use tab to navigate
Mark an address in visual mode with 'mK' and return with "'K"; replace K with your chosen mark key.
Explore a function's basic-block graph with 'agf interactive'.
Use '[' and ']' in visual hexdump mode to adjust the number of columns.
Select your architecture with: 'e asm.arch=<arch>' or rizin -a from the shell
Set 'e scr.nkey=hit' and use 'n' and 'N' in visual mode to seek through search hits.
Save projects with 'Ps <project-filename>' and reopen them with 'Po <project-filename>'.
Enable asm.trace to see the tracing information inside the disassembly
Change the registers of the child process in this way: 'dr eax=0x333'
Check your IO plugins with 'rizin -L'
Resize a file with 'r <size>'; use 'r' alone to display its size.
Calculate checksums for the current block with the commands starting with 'ph' ('ph md5', 'ph crc32', ..)
With the visual cursor disabled, use '%' to toggle automatic block sizing; '+', '-', '*', and '/' adjust the block size when automatic sizing is off.
Change the block size with 'b <block-size>'. In visual mode you can also enter rizin command pressing the ':' key (like vi does)
If you want to open the file in read-write mode, invoke rizin with '-w'
Print the current block as a hexdump with 'px'.
Command layout is: '<repeat><command> <bytes> @ <offset>'.  For example: '3x 20 @ 0x33' will show 3 hexdumps of 20 bytes at 0x33
Use 'el <key>' to read the description of a configuration setting.
Press 'C' in visual mode to toggle colors
You can 'copy/paste' bytes using the cursor in visual mode 'c' and using the 'y' and 'Y' keys
Move around the bytes with h,j,k,l! Arrow keys are neither portable nor efficient
Seek relative to the current address with 'sd <delta>', for example 'sd 16' or 'sd -16'.
Insert a hexadecimal byte block with the 'I' key in visual mode.
Switch between print modes using the 'p' and 'P' keys in visual mode
Add comments using the ';' key in visual mode or the 'CC' command from the rizin shell
Assemble instructions with 'a' in visual mode; press 'A' to open the visual assembler.
Find expanded AES keys with '/cm aes128', '/cm aes192', or '/cm aes256'.
Find UTF-16LE strings with '/z <string> l utf16le'.
Control the signal handlers of the child process with the 'dk' command
List shellcode plugins with 'rz-gg -L'.
Interpret rizin scripts with '. <path-to-script>'. Similar to the bash source alias command.
Most of commands accept '?' as a suffix. Use it to understand how they work :)
Find hexadecimal bytes with '/x a0cc33'.
Step through seek history with 'shu' (undo) and 'shr' (redo).
Use 'p=e' to display a histogram of entropy per block.
Use 'p=p' to display a histogram of printable bytes per block.
Add colors to your screen with 'e scr.color=X' where 1 is 16 colors, 2 is 256 colors and 3 is 16M colors
Align disassembly comments by setting 'e asm.cmt.col=<column>'.
Execute a command on the visual prompt with cmd.vprompt
Use 'fd' to describe the flag and delta at the current address.
Disable these messages with 'e cfg.fortunes=false' in your ~/.rizinrc
Choose a fortune category with 'e cfg.fortunes.file=tips' or 'e cfg.fortunes.file=fun'.
Execute a command every time a breakpoint is hit with 'e cmd.bp=!my-program'
Disassemble in intel syntax with 'e asm.syntax=intel'.
Use 'e graph.layout=1' for a horizontal graph layout, or 0 for a vertical layout.
Set the terminal height with 'e scr.rows=<rows>'.
Use `rizin -B 0x123456 /bin/ls` option to set the base address of a PIE file.
Compare two files in the interactive hexadecimal view with 'rz-diff -H file1 file2'.
Execute commands on a temporary offset by appending '@ offset' to your command.
Temporarily change configuration for one command with '<command> @e:<key>=<value>'.
Control graph node contents with 'e graph.bytes=true' and 'e graph.comments=true'.
Use 'J' and 'K' in visual mode for larger seek steps when the cursor is disabled.
Use 'ix.' to display source line information at the current address.
Use 'ij' to print binary information as JSON.
To debug a program, you can call rizin with 'dbg://<path-to-program>' or '-d <path..>'
Use 'e' in visual mode to edit configuration and 't' to manage tabs.
Use 'rz-bin -iE <binary>' to list imports and exports.
Remember to maintain your `$(rizin -H RZ_CACHEHOME)/history` file!
Set an external pager with 'e scr.pager="less -R"'.
Use 'e asm.segoff=true' for segmented addresses in x86 16-bit mode.
The '%' command can be used to evaluate math expressions. Like this: '% (0x34+22)*4'
Set 'e bin.dbginfo=true' to load debug information at startup.
Rename a function with 'afn <newname> @ <address>'.
Use 'V_' to select flags, comments, and functions from the HUD.
Use 'rz-bin -Ij <binary>' to print binary information as JSON.
Use rz-run to launch your programs with a predefined environment.
Use '-e bin.strings=false' to disable automatic string search when loading the binary.
The unix-like reverse engineering framework.
List supported architectures with 'e asm.arch=?'.
Thanks for using rizin!
give | and > a try piping and redirection
Use 'dmm' to list loaded debugger modules and 'dmi <library>' to list a module's symbols.
Use V! to enter into the visual panels mode (dwm style)
In visual mode, press space to enter the function graph.
Use 'aa' for basic analysis, 'aaa' for deeper analysis, and 'aaaa' for experimental analysis.
Review all the subcommands of aa to see better ways to analyze your targets.
Use '/m' to search for magic signatures; limit the range with 'search.from', 'search.to', and 'search.in'.
You can use registers in math expressions. For example: 'wx 1234 @ esp - 2'
For HTTP authentication 'e http.auth=1', 'e http.authfile=<path>'
Save your project in compressed format with 'e prj.compress=true'
Use 'avg?' to know how you can use global variables in rizin
Inspect an instruction's RzIL with 'aoi' or pretty-print it with 'aoip'.
Use 'La' to list architecture plugins and their analysis, assembly, ESIL, and RzIL support.
Enable 'e io.cache=true' to cache writes; inspect them with 'wc' and commit them with 'wci'.
